WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.277
Idea. Your skeleton is the frame of bones inside your body. In the exam you often see a drawing of some bones, and you must name them. Position is your best clue. Look at the arm. The upper arm has one bone. The forearm, between the elbow and the wrist, has two bones side by side. Then come the wrist, the hand and the thumb.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:32.829
The leg is built in the same way. The thigh has one long bone. The lower leg has two bones. And a small, flat bone sits at the front of the knee. This is the kneecap.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:48.892
Method. Step one: find a landmark, a part you know, like the elbow or the thumb. The upper arm bone is the humerus. In the forearm, use the thumb. The radius is on the thumb side. The ulna is on the little-finger side.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:01:00.051
The finger bones are the phalanges. In the leg, the thigh bone is the femur. Do not swap it with the humerus: the femur is in the leg, the humerus is in the arm.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:08.349
In the lower leg, the large shin bone is the tibia. The thin bone beside it is the fibula. The kneecap is the patella.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:20.394
Now the trunk. The backbone is the vertebral column: a stack of small bones. The clavicle is the collarbone, at the front. The scapula is the shoulder blade, at the back.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:38.369
Some questions show a long bone cut open. Hard, dense compact bone forms the outside of the shaft. Spongy bone, full of small spaces, fills the ends. Marrow, a soft tissue, fills the middle. Cartilage, a smooth, firm tissue, covers the ends.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:56.309
Worked example. Now a real exam question. The diagram is a model of the bones in the arm. Each bone is a black block. Letter A is the thumb. The question asks you to name the bones labelled 1, 2, 3 and 4. It is worth four marks.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:04.439
Here is the same model. Bone 1 is the single block at the top: the upper arm. So bone 1 is the humerus.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:16.400
Bones 2 and 3 are the pair in the forearm. The thumb, A, is on the left. Bone 2 is on the thumb side, so bone 2 is the radius. Bone 3 is the ulna.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:27.825
Label 4 points to the fingers, so bone 4 is the phalanges. Four names, four marks. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:40.863
Common mistake. The most common mistake is swapping the radius and the ulna. Always find the thumb first. Another is calling the upper arm bone the femur. The femur is in the thigh.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:55.133
Spell each name carefully. A made-up word such as tibula mixes tibia and fibula, and gets no mark. And in a bone section, compact bone is the dense outside. Spongy bone is the part with spaces.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:08.828
Your turn. This drawing shows a leg from the side. The person is sitting, so the thigh is flat and the lower leg hangs down. Name bones P, Q, R and S. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:24.383
Answer. P is the single bone from the hip to the knee: the femur. Q is the small bone at the front of the knee: the patella. R is the large bone below the knee: the tibia. S is the thin bone beside it: the fibula.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:33.988
So, every time: find a landmark. Count the bones: one, then two. Use the thumb for the forearm. Then spell each name carefully.
